{#- Atlas — surface one: feature planning on a desk (decisions 22, 23, 24, 25, and #780).

  NOT A TABLE. M1 built this page as an HTML table — features as columns, ladder rows as `<tr>`,
  every intersection a `<td>` — and every visual complaint in #780 followed from that. Cells want
  borders and labels and equal weight, so the arrow became a glyph in a box, finished work became a
  fill with the word "Passed" in it, and future work needed something in it because a cell looks
  broken when it is empty. The owner's framing, and it sat above everything else on the list: it is
  not a chart and they are not grid cells.

  What it is instead: one ribbon per feature descending a shared ladder. The ladder supplies the
  vertical scale; the ribbons are the drawing. Nothing occupies a cell, because there are no cells.

  THIS LAYOUT POSITIONS NOTHING. Every coordinate, every radius and every path comes from
  `src/chart.mjs`, which is pure and unit-tested; this file interpolates them and does no
  arithmetic of its own. That is the same rule M1's version followed for the two ends of a column,
  applied to the whole picture — a position computed in a template is a position no unit test can
  reach, and a rendered page is a slow and blunt place to discover it is wrong.

  The claim above was FALSE in the first draft, and nothing caught it: the ribbon body's left edge
  was written `lane.centre - 18` while its width was read from `CHART.ribbonWidth`, so raising that
  constant moved the arrowhead and left the body four pixels off-centre behind it with the whole
  suite green. `tests/theme.test.mjs` now reads every measurement back out of the rendered markup
  and compares it against the drawing, so a number written here rather than interpolated fails the
  moment the constant it duplicates is changed. The only literals left below are `0`, an origin,
  and the percentages a filter region is defined in.

  Two drawings, side by side inside one horizontal scroller:

    * the LADDER gutter, which is `position: sticky; left: 0` so it stays put while
    * the LANES scroll sideways under it.

  A lane is drawn about its own origin and placed with a single `transform`, so `theme/order.js`
  can move a whole feature — ribbon, dots, dates, skip marker and balloon together — by rewriting
  one number, with no geometry on the client at all.

  Data this layout expects:
    chart — computeChart(ladder, workstreams)'s output: rows, bands, rules and lanes
    project, repo, title — see base.njk
-#}
{% extends "base.njk" %}
{% import "base.njk" as ui %}

{% block bodyClass %}page page-planning{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<h1>Feature planning</h1>
<p class="lede">One ribbon per feature, from the top of the ladder through the stages and on into the milestones. A solid arrow covers what has begun; where more is on record than has been started, a second, fainter arrow reaches as far as the records go. Both end in their own head.</p>

{#- The owner's own request, and he knows what it is: an order remembered on THIS DEVICE. The page
    says so in as many words rather than letting a reader discover it on their phone. -#}
<div class="planning-order">
<p class="order-note" id="order-note">Drag a feature's header to put the features in the order you want, or focus one and press the left and right arrow keys. <strong>The order is remembered on this device only</strong> — it does not follow you to another browser, or to your phone. An order that travelled with you would have to be written back to the repository, which Atlas does not yet do.</p>
<button type="button" class="order-reset" data-order-reset hidden>Back to the generated order</button>
{#- Moving a feature with the arrow keys changes a `transform` on an SVG group, which a screen
    reader has no reason to announce. This is where the move is said out loud instead. -#}
<p class="order-said" data-order-said role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
</div>

<div class="chart-scroll" data-planning-scroll>
<svg class="planning-ladder" width="{{ chart.ladderWidth }}" height="{{ chart.height }}" viewBox="0 0 {{ chart.ladderWidth }} {{ chart.height }}" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
<rect class="ladder-ground" x="0" y="0" width="{{ chart.ladderWidth }}" height="{{ chart.height }}"/>
{%- for band in chart.bands %}{% if band.id == "execution" %}
<text class="band-name" x="{{ band.labelX }}" y="{{ band.labelY }}" transform="rotate(-90 {{ band.labelX }} {{ band.labelY }})" text-anchor="middle">{{ band.label }}</text>
{%- endif %}{% endfor %}
{%- for row in chart.rows %}
<text class="{% if row.kind == "stage" %}lad-stage{% else %}lad-num num{% endif %}" x="{{ chart.ladderCaptionX }}" y="{{ row.captionY }}" text-anchor="end">{{ row.caption }}</text>
{%- endfor %}
<line class="ladder-edge" x1="{{ chart.ladderWidth }}" y1="0" x2="{{ chart.ladderWidth }}" y2="{{ chart.ladderBottom }}"/>
</svg>

<svg class="planning-chart" width="{{ chart.lanesWidth }}" height="{{ chart.height }}" viewBox="0 0 {{ chart.lanesWidth }} {{ chart.height }}" data-planning-chart data-column-pitch="{{ chart.columnPitch }}" role="group" aria-label="One ribbon per feature down a shared ladder of stages and milestones. Every feature is also listed in full on the triage view.">
<defs>
<filter id="balloon-shadow" x="-30%" y="-40%" width="160%" height="200%"><feDropShadow dx="0" dy="3" stdDeviation="4" flood-color="#0f172a" flood-opacity="0.16"/></filter>
</defs>

{#- The four bands (#780). They belong to the ladder rather than to any feature, so the phase reads
    horizontally across every feature at once — the horizontal reading is the phase, the vertical
    is the individual feature's path. -#}
<g class="bands">
{%- for band in chart.bands %}
<rect class="band band-{{ band.id }}" x="0" y="{{ band.y }}" width="{{ chart.lanesWidth }}" height="{{ band.height }}"/>
{%- endfor %}
</g>

{#- A light rule at every ladder row, drawn BENEATH the ribbons, so a ribbon crosses a rule rather
    than being cut by it. Lines, not a grid: the moment these meet verticals they are cells again. -#}
<g class="rules">
{%- for y in chart.rules %}
<line class="rule" x1="0" y1="{{ y }}" x2="{{ chart.lanesWidth }}" y2="{{ y }}"/>
{%- endfor %}
</g>

<g class="lanes" data-lanes>
{%- for lane in chart.lanes %}
<g class="lane" data-slug="{{ lane.slug }}" transform="translate({{ lane.x }},0)">

{#- The header is the drag handle, and it is focusable, because drag alone is not enough: the
    arrow keys move a feature too. -#}
<g class="lane-head" data-lane-head data-name="{{ lane.codename }}" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-describedby="order-note" aria-label="{{ lane.codename }}. Press the left and right arrow keys to move this feature.">
<rect class="lane-head-box" x="{{ chart.head.x }}" y="{{ chart.head.y }}" width="{{ chart.head.width }}" height="{{ chart.head.height }}" rx="{{ chart.head.radius }}" ry="{{ chart.head.radius }}"/>
<rect class="lane-head-accent" x="{{ chart.head.x }}" y="{{ chart.head.y }}" width="{{ chart.head.accentWidth }}" height="{{ chart.head.height }}" rx="{{ chart.head.accentRadius }}" ry="{{ chart.head.accentRadius }}"/>
<a href="{{ lane.url }}"><text class="col-h" x="{{ chart.head.textX }}" y="{{ chart.head.titleY }}">{{ lane.codename }}</text></a>
{{ ui.svgChip("stage", lane.stage, chart.head.textX, chart.head.chipY) }}
</g>

{#- The ribbon. ONE OBJECT PER FEATURE, SEEN TWICE. The faint arrow spans the whole recorded length
    of the feature; the solid arrow is laid OVER it, as far as the work has actually reached. They
    overlay — M2.1 tiled them, solid stopping where faint began, and #780 corrected that on sight.

    DRAW ORDER IS THE OVERLAY. SVG has no z-index: the painter's order is the document order, so
    the faint arrow is emitted FIRST and the solid one on top of it. Swapping these two blocks
    paints a pale bar over the work, and nothing in `src/chart.mjs` can catch it — which is why
    `tests/theme.test.mjs` asserts the order of the elements themselves.

    Each arrow ends in its own head, growing straight out of its own body — nothing floats, and
    there is never a gap between body and head. -#}
<g class="ribbon">
{%- if lane.faint %}
{%- for segment in lane.faint.segments %}
<rect class="ribbon-body tone-{{ segment.tone }}" x="{{ lane.faint.x }}" y="{{ segment.y }}" width="{{ lane.faint.width }}" height="{{ segment.height }}" rx="{{ lane.faint.radius }}" ry="{{ lane.faint.radius }}"/>
{%- endfor %}
{%- for d in lane.faint.detours %}
<path class="ribbon-detour tone-ahead" d="{{ d }}" fill="none"/>
{%- endfor %}
<path class="ribbon-head tone-{{ lane.faint.head.tone }}" d="{{ lane.faint.head.d }}"/>
{%- endif %}
{%- for segment in lane.solid.segments %}
<rect class="ribbon-body tone-{{ segment.tone }}" x="{{ lane.solid.x }}" y="{{ segment.y }}" width="{{ lane.solid.width }}" height="{{ segment.height }}" rx="{{ lane.solid.radius }}" ry="{{ lane.solid.radius }}"/>
{%- endfor %}
{%- for d in lane.solid.detours %}
<path class="ribbon-detour" d="{{ d }}" fill="none"/>
{%- endfor %}
<path class="ribbon-head tone-{{ lane.solid.head.tone }}" d="{{ lane.solid.head.d }}"/>
</g>

{#- A dot per milestone the ribbon covers, with its stored days beside it. Nothing below the final
    arrowhead, whatever its status — the rule is positional, not status-based (#780). -#}
{%- for dot in lane.dots %}
<g class="milestone-dot tone-{{ dot.tone }}">
<circle class="dot{% if dot.hollow %} dot-hollow{% endif %}" cx="{{ lane.centre }}" cy="{{ dot.y }}" r="{{ dot.r }}"/>
{%- for line in dot.lines %}
<text class="{% if line.strong %}dt-b num{% else %}dt{% endif %}" x="{{ lane.textX }}" y="{{ line.y }}">{{ line.text }}</text>
{%- endfor %}
</g>
{%- endfor %}

{#- A skipped milestone is noted, not treated as a wall. The ribbon has already left its lane
    around this marker; here is the marker, and the reason to read it at. -#}
{%- for skip in lane.skips %}
<g class="skip-mark">
<circle class="skip-ring" cx="{{ lane.centre }}" cy="{{ skip.y }}" r="{{ skip.r }}"/>
<path class="skip-cross" d="{{ skip.cross }}" fill="none"/>
<text class="skip-t" x="{{ lane.textX }}" y="{{ skip.labelY }}">{{ skip.label }}</text>
<text class="dt" x="{{ lane.textX }}" y="{{ skip.reasonY }}">{{ skip.reason }}</text>
</g>
{%- endfor %}

{#- The balloon, at the step it describes rather than at the end of the arrow. Fixed width tied to
    the column, growing downward as the text needs; its connector steps into a lane just clear of
    this feature's own ribbon and travels vertically inside this column, so it cannot cross a
    neighbour. No next step, no balloon. -#}
{%- if lane.balloon %}
<g class="balloon tone-{{ lane.balloon.tone }}">
<path class="balloon-line" d="{{ lane.balloon.connector }}" fill="none"/>
<circle class="balloon-pin" cx="{{ lane.balloon.dot.x }}" cy="{{ lane.balloon.dot.y }}" r="{{ lane.balloon.dot.r }}"/>
<path class="balloon-body" d="{{ lane.balloon.path }}" filter="url(#balloon-shadow)"/>
<text class="bl-k" x="{{ lane.balloon.textX }}" y="{{ lane.balloon.kickerY }}">{{ lane.balloon.kicker }}</text>
{%- for line in lane.balloon.lines %}
<text class="bl-t" x="{{ lane.balloon.textX }}" y="{{ line.y }}">{{ line.text }}</text>
{%- endfor %}
</g>
{%- endif %}

</g>
{%- endfor %}
</g>
</svg>
</div>

<ul class="chart-key">
<li><span class="key-swatch tone-done"></span>finished</li>
<li><span class="key-swatch tone-live"></span>in progress</li>
<li><span class="key-swatch tone-ahead"></span>on record, not started</li>
<li><span class="key-swatch key-skip"></span>skipped — the work went round it</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block bodyScripts %}
<script type="module" src="/order.js"></script>
{% endblock %}
